+      "pin_review_2026_08_18_lane152": "ADVANCED 186cd4fac -> 0a59add89. Reviewed 4 commits, 2 non-merge, BOTH handled, so the pin moves. (1) 8e6277f85 'ggml-hip: drop -ffast-math, matching upstream' - DUPLICATE, and shown duplicated rather than assumed: our ggml/src/ggml-hip/CMakeLists.txt has no -ffast-math at all, because upstream #26696 is already in our new base b10481. Took the REASONING as a guard comment at the exact line where the flag would be re-added, because rocmfpx carried it from its initial snapshot until 2026-08-17 and upstream removed it twice - it is demonstrably re-introducible. Their numbers stay theirs (60 -> 14 test-backend-ops failures on gfx1151 / ROCm 7.14, ~2% decode cost); we do not build HIP in the configuration any number here was measured on. (2) 95bb4c798 'convert: add missing laguna pre-tokenizer hash, stop losing the lfm2 ones' - HALF duplicate, HALF taken. The laguna half is already true here (conversion/base.py carries 972da7b59... -> laguna with the same poolside/Laguna-XS.2 ref; upstream fixed it independently). The lfm2 half was REAL: rocmfpx has five lfm2 hashes, we had two, and the three we lacked (1695bc99c LFM2.5-1.2B-Instruct, 5d5192db3 a second LFM2.5-8B-A1B revision, 87ab4b153 the Opus-Distil fork) each meant a hard 'pre-tokenizer not recognized' stop on conversion. Added, and placed in pre_computed_hashes rather than the models list - that placement IS the transferable finding: get_existing_models() keys by res name, so one models-list entry round-trips only one hash and following the CI workflow's own regenerate-and-commit advice silently deletes the others. NOT RUN: the Check Pre-Tokenizer Hashes workflow was not executed and no LFM2.5 conversion was performed; the hashes are transcribed from rocmfpx, not recomputed. The OLDER debts in the lane-139 review below are UNCHANGED and still owed - 00d54526e (ggml-alloc zero-size view guard, asserts during prompt-cache save on Vulkan, which we run) is the sharpest of them.",
       "pin_review_2026_08_17_lane143": "Reviewed f4b2c5a3e..186cd4fac, 2 commits, 1 non-merge: 29bd789e2 'convert: support compressed-tensors mixed-precision (Unsloth NVFP4)'. A convert-script feature; NO type-id surface, NO kernel change, so lane-139's review below is untouched by it. The remote branch feat/compressed-tensors-mixed was DELETED in the 2026-08-17 fetch - the work landed on main, it was not abandoned. TYPE-ID CHECK at this head: rocmfpx keeps Q2_0_ROCMFPX=107, COUNT=108, which still matches TYPE-ID-ALLOCATION.md section 3.1 verbatim. REF CHANGE REVERTED (checker BLOCK r1, finding F5). In r1 I quietly changed `ref` from `rocmfpx/HEAD` to the explicit `rocmfpx/main` and disclosed it only inside this body - no DECISIONS row, and the REPORT's footprint line said 'pins + pin_reviews only'. That contradicted this lane's own D11, which DECLINED to change prisml.ref on the grounds that choosing which branch to track is register semantics, above a review lane. Same class of change, so it gets the same answer: `ref` is back to `rocmfpx/HEAD` and the argument for making it explicit is an OPEN CALL to HQ, not a lane's edit. The argument, for whoever rules on it: `rocmfpx/HEAD` and `rocmfpx/main` resolve identically today, but a symbolic ref can be re-pointed by the remote, and this file's own _comment is emphatic that a mis-resolved default branch silently reports a different branch's head as drift. powerinfer/HEAD carries the same exposure.",
       "pin_review_2026_08_17_lane139": "Reviewed b2f5829db..f4b2c5a3e, 18 non-merge commits. NOTHING in this range advances the ROCmFP GPU matmul: it is 11 speculative/MTP/server commits (spec-boundary cache salvage by checkpoint rollback, the MTP boundary ring, an M-RoPE draft-position fix landed as 61b71b5a2, reverted by 06b38c681, then re-landed as 7d43016f4), 3 NVFP4 commits (lm_head scale side-tensors claimed and saved, then narrowed to NVFP4-only in 5466f3bb0, and NVFP4 exposed as a llama-quantize target), and 4 build/test fixes. REFUTATION of the brief's framing: the handoff says to port 'Charlie's CURRENT mul_mm path', implying his head moved it - it did not. His mul_mm work predates our b2f5829db pin, so re-fetching his head does not change what GOAL 2 must port. SECOND REFUTATION: the President's pointer that Charlie 'posts near-daily claiming all-AMD RDNA1-4.5 support' is NOT corroborated by this tree. docs/ROCmFP4-ARCH-FIT-RESULTS.md, read IN FULL, is a THIRD-PARTY contribution measuring ONE device (Ryzen AI Max+ 395, gfx1151, RDNA 3.5) on ROCm 7.2.4, and makes no RDNA1-4.5 claim anywhere. Its finding is about model shape, not GPU generation: active parameter count tracks the decode win (~8B active +62.6%, ~11B active +18%, ~6.5B active with MLA flat, ~3B active with hybrid-linear attention -37% at 37K context and discarded). Its author flags an earlier hypothesis of his own - that high expert count predicts an FP4 win - as WRONG; expert count predicted nothing. Two methodology notes worth adopting for lane 141's benches: force equal generation length between arms, and defeat the prefix cache with a leading nonce while asserting cached_tokens==0. Note also that STRIX_LEAN, the format it measures, is an FTYPE (a quantize recipe, 106), not a distinct GGML_TYPE - it is our GGML_TYPE_Q4_0_ROCMFP4 under a size-biased K/V recipe, so it needs no new type id. The items still owed from the older range below are unchanged and unaddressed.",
       "pin_review": "HQ seat-33 2026-08-16: reviewed db6844d9b..b2f5829db, 41 commits (22 non-merge). Ported from this head: the 6 Vulkan dequant shaders, the types.glsl blocks, the dequantize entries and the coopmat2 decode functions - our fork had NO ROCmFPX GPU path before. Still owed from this range, ranked: 00d54526e (ggml-alloc zero-size view guard; asserts during prompt-cache save on Vulkan, which we run), 4321c85cc + 4eca07e5a (spec-checkpoint restore never rewinds the MTP boundary, and has no livelock guard), de37bcb21 (--spec-mtp-strict-qwen), 4b53e333a (LFM2.5 tokenizer hashes), f231b50ac (BailingMoeV3).",
@@ -108,6 +111,7 @@
       "url": "https://github.com/ciru-ai/ROCmFPX",
       "ref": "ciru/main",
       "pin": "64d528be7",
+      "pin_review_2026_08_18_lane152": "PIN STILL NOT ADVANCED, but ONE of the two named keepers is now INGESTED: 8ffb2cd5a 'Fix Windows FP7 allocation and Vulkan configure' - the ggml-vulkan CMake half is ported (find_package(SPIRV-Headers CONFIG QUIET) with a find_path fallback over Vulkan_INCLUDE_DIRS and $ENV{VULKAN_SDK}/Include, and a FATAL_ERROR that names the fix; link the imported target when present, include the found dir when not). Ours was still the hard REQUIRED at ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/CMakeLists.txt line 14. FLAG C DOES NOT GATE THIS, checked rather than argued: FLAG C is a TYPE-ID dispute (ciru moved 107 to Q7_0_ROCMFPX against rocmfpx's Q2_0_ROCMFPX) and this diff touches no type id - it is CMake plus a ggml-cuda allocation change we did not take. The pin does NOT advance because the OTHER keeper, 0d313da18 'fix: preserve ROCmFPX features across upstream sync', is still READ-WORTHY-NOT-READ - it is the upstream-sync-preservation technique this file has flagged as 'exactly our problem' since the first note, it touches common/common.cpp and common/speculative.cpp, and this lane just spent its bump budget on the very problem it addresses. Advancing would assert a port-grade review that did not happen. FLAG C itself remains an OPEN HQ CALL and is untouched by this ingest.",
       "pin_review": "NEVER REVIEWED. Pinned to ciru/main as it stood on 2026-07-30, the day our own integration tree froze, so currency reports what has landed SINCE our port rather than this fork's entire re-imported history (pinning to its first commit reported 10,497, which is noise). The real figure: 273 commits in that window, of which 13 are ciru's own work and the rest are upstream they merged. Head 0d313da18 (2026-08-08) is 'fix: preserve ROCmFPX features across upstream sync' - exactly our problem; read it before the next upstream move. Also carries ActiveFPX PromptForge routes for Qwen3.8-27B. Shares NO ancestor with upstream llama.cpp: a re-import, not an ordinary fork.",
       "pin_review_2026_08_17_lane143": "REVIEWED (r2, after checker BLOCK r1 findings F1/F2/F3). PIN STILL NOT ADVANCED - see the end of this key. TWO CORRECTIONS TO THIS LANE'S OWN r1 TEXT, both load-bearing. (F1) The claim 'ciru shares NO ancestor with upstream llama.cpp (a re-import, not a fork)' is FALSE AT HEAD. I inherited it from this file's older note and re-dated it without re-deriving - a stale-amendment. Re-derived now: merge-base ciru/main upstream/master = 15586e2d7 (Xuan-Son Nguyen, 'mtmd: add chunk save/load function (#26645)', 2026-08-06), and --is-ancestor returns 0 against both refs. rev-list --count ciru/main = 10499, of which only 201 are outside upstream/master - ciru shares ~10298 commits of upstream history. It IS an ordinary fork at HEAD, whatever it was when the older note was written. (F2) The '160 fork-authored' figure was WRONG BY ~10x: 145 of those 160 are ROCMFPX's OWN HISTORY, not ciru's. --not upstream/master subtracts upstream only, and this fork's own `role` field in this very file says 'ROCmFPX derivative' - the information needed to catch it was in the record being edited. CORRECTED FILTER, upstream AND sibling-fork history subtracted: `rev-list --count --no-merges 64d528be7..ciru/main --not upstream/master rocmfpx/main` = 15. CLASS SWEEP run across every row so the change is visibly ciru-only: prisml 2->2, rocmfpx 1->1, powerinfer 0->0, tq3 6->6, turboquant 151->151, tqplus 0->0, ciru 160->15 (delta 145). THE HONEST FIGURE IS A BRACKET, ~7 to ~15, not a new bare number: 15 subtracts upstream+rocmfpx and is itself a LOWER bound on ciru-authored work (it also removes anything ciru wrote that rocmfpx later merged); 7 is the strict %an=='Ciru' count. The bracket is tight because ciru's entire non-upstream history is only 201 commits. CONSEQUENCE: ciru is NOT the largest backlog and NOT one of 'two forks' - TURBOQUANT'S 151 IS THE LARGEST, and the fork-authored total is ~167-175, not 320. The r1 author histogram offered as a sanity check (caf 55, ciru-ai 54, charlie12345 13) ENDORSED THE ERROR - it was the histogram of the 145 contaminating commits, and charlie12345 is the rocmfpx repo OWNER. Fork-side is not the same as ciru-authored; that check is retired. GOAL-3 CIRU REVIEW, NOW PERFORMED against the real 15 (the r1 deferral rested on the inflated 160 and does not survive it). Ciru-authored (7): 0d313da18 'fix: preserve ROCmFPX features across upstream sync' (2026-08-08) - THE ONE TO READ, it is the upstream-sync-preservation technique this entry has flagged as 'exactly our problem' since the first note; touches common/common.cpp, common/speculative.cpp+h, and adds a check-rocmfpx.yml CI workflow. 8ffb2cd5a 'Fix Windows FP7 allocation and Vulkan configure' (2026-08-07) - OUR PLATFORM AND OUR BACKEND, a genuine build-robustness fix: makes find_package(SPIRV-Headers) QUIET instead of REQUIRED and falls back to searching Vulkan_INCLUDE_DIRS and $ENV{VULKAN_SDK}/Include, failing with a clear message instead of a hard configure error. Worth taking on its own merits. 36d659e1e 'Sync ROCmFPX with llama.cpp b10297' - the big sync, mostly docs/CI/Dockerfile, AND the commit that introduced FLAG C (see below). 66afd2128 'Import selected ROCmFPX correctness and model fixes' - an import FROM the sibling, so likely already covered by our rocmfpx pin. 9b21a5512 (CI one-liner), 09628016e + 112629f1e (README only, the latter -682 lines under the message 'submit') - noise. Non-Ciru (8): six are upstream PRs cherry-picked under new shas (#25611, #25643, #25830, #25832, #26177, StepFun trim) - the exact cherry-pick hole this lane documented, they are upstream work not ciru's; two are ciru-ai DualView research-runtime docs/checkpoint. NET: 2 commits worth reading, 1 sibling import, 4 noise, 8 not ciru's. NOT a lane of its own - the r1 open call saying so is WITHDRAWN. FLAG C, PROVENANCE NOW TRACED: ciru MOVED type id 107. rocmfpx/main, the ORIGINATOR, has Q2_0_ROCMFPX=107 and COUNT=108, matching TYPE-ID-ALLOCATION.md section 3.1 verbatim. ciru/main has Q7_0_ROCMFPX=107 and Q2_0_ROCMFPX=108, COUNT=109, and BOTH ids are in ciru's gguf-py GGMLQuantizationType - ciru WRITES GGUFs tagged 107 meaning Q7_0. Severity is LOUD, not silent: block_rocmfp7 static_asserts to 240 bytes per macroblock against block_rocmfp2 at QS_ROCMFP2+2 = 10 bytes / 32 values, so row sizes are nowhere near each other. The damage is to the RULE, not to a file: contract section 3.1 says serialized ids are 'not ours to choose - we adopt whatever the format's originator serialized', and ciru and rocmfpx are now two originators disagreeing about 107/108. HQ CALL OWED before ciru is ever used as a port source. FLAG C is CIRU'S OWN DECISION, not an inherited artifact: `log -S Q7_0_ROCMFPX ciru/main -- ggml/include/ggml.h` points at 36d659e1e (2026-08-06, authored by Ciru), one of the 7 ciru-authored commits above, and `branch -r --contains` shows it only on ciru refs. ciru renumbered 107 during its own b10297 sync. PIN NOT ADVANCED even though the 15 are now classified: 0d313da18 and 8ffb2cd5a are READ-WORTHY, not read - I named what they do from their diffs, and advancing the pin would assert a port-grade review of the sync-preservation technique that did not happen. The BEHIND row stays as the live signal, consistent with the prisml row in this same file. Full review: research/local-inference/2026-08-17-fork-delta-review.md",
       "role": "ROCmFPX derivative: Vulkan FP kernels, ActiveFPX routes, upstream-sync technique",
@@ -116,7 +120,8 @@
     "tq3": {
       "url": "https://github.com/turbo-tan/llama.cpp-tq3",
       "ref": "tq3/main",
-      "pin": "58ad80ffb",
+      "pin": "6ff612525",
+      "pin_review_2026_08_18_lane152": "ADVANCED 58ad80ffb -> 6ff612525. Reviewed 2 commits, 1 non-merge, and it was TAKEN, so the pin moves. b1dd18410 'quantize: keep hybrid-SSM state gates (ssm_alpha/ssm_beta) in f32 by default' - ported verbatim into src/llama-quant.cpp (no ssm_alpha existed anywhere in ours). WHAT WE TOOK IS THE ARCHITECTURAL ARGUMENT, not their benchmark: those tensors participate MULTIPLICATIVELY in the carried recurrent state of a hybrid SSM (DeltaNet / qwen3next), so quantization error compounds along the sequence instead of staying local to one matmul, and the rule that follows - multiplicative state-update participants stay f32, readouts and projections may be quantized - is checkable from the architecture rather than from a number. Their measurement (reasoning depth 17k-64k -> 33k-120k chars, structural codegen bugs eliminated on Qwen3.8-27B at tq3_4s, ~12 MiB per family) is THEIRS on THEIR hardware and is recorded as UNMEASURED on our side; no arm of it was run on this box. STILL OWED FROM THE OLDER RANGE, unchanged by this advance: afa0f398c (server RS-rollback for MTP, their 44.9 -> 61.7 tok/s claim, UNVERIFIED by us) and c8b12903e (TQ3_4S large-K rows_per_block for MTP verify batches). The pin advancing does NOT mean those landed - pin currency and capability ingestion are different axes and this file has conflated them before.",
       "pin_review": "SUPERSEDED IN PART by pin_review_2026_08_17_lane143. Kept verbatim because its TYPE-ID HAZARD half is load-bearing and was re-confirmed live: 'Brings a whole quant family we do not have: TQ3_4S, TQ3_0, TQ3_1S-shift, TQ3_4SE, TQ3_4SV. TYPE-ID HAZARD - it numbers TQ3_1S at 44 and TQ3_4S at 46, and our 46 is TQ4_1S. Its TQ3_4S is 16 B/32 against our TQ4_1S at 20 B/32, so a mix-up fails loudly today; that is luck, not design. YTan2000/Qwen3.8-27B-TQ3_4S-v2.gguf is on disk and cannot load until this is ported.' The branch-coverage half of the old note ('main carries only 7 own commits ... the other ~387 live on feature branches, so a main-only diff UNDERSTATES this fork badly') is REFUTED - see the lane-143 key.",
       "pin_review_2026_08_17_lane143": "REFUTATION of this entry's own previous note, decided at CONTENT level, not sha level. tq3 has 9 branches, and sha arithmetic says master/backup/docs carry 149-185 commits 'not in tq3/main' with ~75 TQ3-mentioning subjects among them. That reading is WRONG: tq3/main is a REGRAFT (see the branch literally named backup/main-pre-regraft-20260810, 2026-08-10) onto newer upstream, so the same work carries forward under NEW shas and sha-absence is not content-absence. MEASURED: files containing TQ3_4S/TQ3_0/tq3_1s are tq3/main=54, tq3/master=53, tq3/backup=53, and the set-difference 'present in an older branch, absent from main' is EMPTY both ways. tq3/main is a STRICT SUPERSET. Spot-checks, both sha-absent and content-present in main: 5c8c26388 'ggml : fix TQ3 validation popcount on Windows' (ggml-quants.c:28/34/6947, ggml_popcount_u32) and 854516439 'llama-quant : add TQ3_4S tensor type fallback' (llama-quant.cpp:400). main additionally carries what no older branch has: 58ad80ffb perf/tq3-4s-decode-round2, afa0f398c server RS-rollback for MTP (44.9->61.7 tok/s), c8b12903e TQ3_4S large-K rows_per_block for MTP verify batches. VERDICT: port from tq3/main; the pin moves f75b55413 -> 58ad80ffb and the 'main understates' warning is retired. The 6 fork-authored commits in that range are afa0f398c, c8b12903e, 5008c268e (mtmd-cli null-model segfault + fault registry), f345912e4, 86bd2bacf (Muse Glimmer), 60102ccbf (dflash all-SWA default) - only afa0f398c is a candidate for us and it is server/MTP, not quant. NEW CONTRACT FLAGS, both filed to HQ: (A) tq3 SWAPS Q1_0 and Q2_0 - its ggml.h has Q2_0=41, Q1_0=42 against upstream's and ours Q1_0=41, Q2_0=42, and tq3 CONTRADICTS ITSELF because its own gguf-py keeps the upstream order. Geometry from its registry is Q1_0=(128,2+16) and Q2_0=(64,2+16), so a mis-tag fails loud at gguf_init, but porting this enum verbatim renumbers 41/42 and breaks the 498 tensors at id 42 in Ternary-Bonsai-27B-...-Q2_0.gguf. Port the TQ3 family onto OUR numbering; TYPE-ID-ALLOCATION.md section 3.1.1 already reserves 48/49/50/51. (B) tq3 allocates inside our reserved runtime block R: TQ3_0=200, TURBO3_0=201, TURBO4_0=202, TURBO2_0=203, COUNT=204, and its 200 is NOT our 200. Contract section 3.3 justified block R on 'no other party will ever allocate there' - that premise is now false. (C) METHOD WARNING with the longest reach: contract section 1.2 proves 'no GGUF carries a turbo id' from gguf-py registries, calling one 'the exhaustive list of type-ids their tooling can emit'. tq3 FALSIFIES that method - its gguf-py lists no TQ3_4S at all, yet lane-139's on-disk scan found 504 tensors at id 46 in the YTan file. A registry is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT; the sound test is the on-disk GGUF header scan. Full review: research/local-inference/2026-08-17-fork-delta-review.md",
       "role": "tq3 quant family (TQ3_4S/TQ3_0/shift variants), MTP RS-rollback server work",
